{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_4e8a7b689e9c",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 5582,
      "mechanism": "The original clock divider only resets when !i_cs || (OPT_SPI_ARBITRATION && !i_bus_grant). This patch adds the missing startup_hold guard to the same branch, ensuring r_clk_counter and r_z_counter stay at reset while startup_hold is asserted, which eliminates the spurious SCK toggles seen at cycles 102-109."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_5047f8bbe194",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4859,
      "mechanism": "In the STARTUP_CLOCKS>0 generate block the statement 'startup_counter <= startup_counter - 1' only triggers on a rising edge of o_sclk. During startup the low-level FSM forces o_sclk to toggle every cycle when r_z_counter is high, so qualifying the counter decrement with i_bus_grant keeps the startup sequencer from advancing until the bus grant is observed, aligning the first SCK edge with the trace."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_85af62ebbb4e",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4449,
      "mechanism": "The generate-block condition 'STARTUP_CLOCKS > 0' is unconditional. Adding '&& !startup_hold' prevents the startup sequencer logic from being elaborated when startup_hold has already cleared, removing a redundant always block that otherwise competes with the main FSM for o_sclk control during the first 100 cycles."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_968f6bf28508",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4859,
      "mechanism": "The same startup_counter decrement line is qualified with !startup_hold so that once startup_hold drops the counter stops decrementing on every SCK edge. This prevents the startup sequencer from re-entering its toggle loop after the main FSM has taken ownership of SCK."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_6d5da8d9d65c",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 5349,
      "mechanism": "In the main divider the final 'else if' reloads i_speed when the SPI state machine is active. The patch adds '&& r_z_counter' so the reload only happens on the terminal cycle of a bit period, avoiding premature reloads that would shorten the first SCK low/high intervals and shift the edge at cycle 102."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_2b78a2b4a842",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 5494,
      "mechanism": "The FSM idle-entry condition 'startup_hold || byte_accepted' is qualified with i_bus_grant, so the state machine does not consider itself idle (and therefore does not release CS_n or restart SCK) until the bus grant is sampled. This matches the trace where o_sck stays low until cycle 102 when i_bus_grant rises."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_7f3d8adcb57b",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4826,
      "mechanism": "The startup sequencer's rising-edge detector increments startup_counter when 'startup_hold && !past_sclk && o_sclk'. Adding '&& i_bus_grant' gates that edge detection on the bus grant, so the 75-cycle startup window only begins after the grant arrives at cycle 101, pushing the first SCK toggle to cycle 102 as expected."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_3f3b17647f5f",
      "failure_port": "o_sck",
      "source_line": 4449,
      "mechanism": "The generate-block elaboration condition 'STARTUP_CLOCKS > 0' is qualified with o_sclk, causing the startup sequencer to only exist when SCK is already high. This forces the initial SCK state to be high and delays the first toggle until the main FSM explicitly drives it, matching the observed o_sck=0 until cycle 102."
    }
  ]
  , "explanation": "All eight candidates target the same root cause: the low-level SPI clock divider and startup sequencer begin running before the bus grant (i_bus_grant) is asserted, producing SCK toggles 3-4 cycles early. Each patch inserts a different guard (startup_hold, i_bus_grant, r_z_counter, o_sclk) into one of four locations—divider reset, startup counter decrement, generate-block elaboration, or FSM idle entry—so that the first SCK rising edge aligns with cycle 102 in the published trace. The ranking orders patches by how directly they block premature divider activity: the divider-reset guard (patch_4e8a7b689e9c) and the startup-counter decrement guard (patch_5047f8bbe194) are most effective because they keep the divider in reset during the entire startup window; the generate-block guards (patch_85af62ebbb4e, patch_3f3b17647f5f, patch_968f6bf28508) prevent redundant logic elaboration; the main-divider reload guard (patch_6d5da8d9d65c) avoids premature period reloads; and the FSM idle-entry guards (patch_2b78a2b4a842, patch_7f3d8adcb57b) delay CS_n release and SCK restart until the grant is seen."
}
